No. The reported contract provisions may support **payer-specific conversion after required review**, but they do not validate the blanket automatic status change in the draft.

“Allow conversion after 24 hours” may mean any of the following, which are not equivalent:

- conversion is permitted only when medical-necessity criteria are met;
- prior authorization or payer notification is required;
- the payer recognizes inpatient reimbursement after a specified threshold;
- conversion is prospective rather than retroactive;
- a practitioner’s inpatient order remains required.

The controlling language, amendments, provider manuals incorporated by reference, and authorization rules must be checked for each payer and product. Commercial contracts also do not alter Medicare requirements or generally eliminate practitioner-order, documentation, state-law, accreditation, or hospital medical-staff requirements.

Use a payer-specific review pathway:

> For commercial observation stays reaching 24 elapsed hours, the system shall generate an expedited utilization-management review under the patient’s applicable payer contract and product requirements. Any conversion to inpatient status must satisfy the contract’s conversion criteria, authorization or notification provisions, practitioner-order requirements, documented medical necessity, and other applicable legal and institutional requirements. Elapsed time alone shall not change patient status. Medicare cases shall follow applicable Medicare requirements.

**Recommendation:** automate the 24-hour review and payer-rule identification, not the inpatient status change. Have counsel map each quoted contract clause to payer, product, prerequisites, effective date, and whether conversion is prospective or retroactive before rollout.

**Assessment: major rewrite of Section 3.1—not minor tweaks, but not necessarily the entire policy.**

The section’s central mechanism is unsupported as written:

- automatic inpatient conversion based on 24 elapsed hours;
- one workflow for Medicare and commercial products;
- patient-access staff changing clinical status;
- reimbursement stated as the purpose;
- post-billing rather than active-stay utilization review;
- immediate claims submission at 48 hours.

The usable concept is the **time-based escalation workflow**. Keep the 24-hour and 48-hour triggers, but make them trigger review, billing holds, notices, discharge planning, and payer-specific validation—not automatic conversion or submission.

A concise steering-committee description:

> Section 3.1 requires substantive revision before rollout. We recommend retaining automated 24- and 48-hour escalation points while replacing automatic status conversion with timely utilization-management review, an authorized-practitioner order when inpatient admission is appropriate, documented clinical and payer-specific criteria, required notices or authorizations, and pre-submission claim validation. Medicare and commercial pathways should be separated.

**Recommendation:** do not approve the current section. Present a replacement section and a payer-by-product requirements matrix for counsel, compliance, utilization management, and revenue-cycle approval.
